.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_scoped \- declare and create a hash map that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro. The macro then creates the map with the default allocator and the
map's own hashing for key_t, and attaches the cleanup attribute, which
destroys the map at every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return
included.

This macro is chmap_construct(3) plus that cleanup attribute, and nothing
else is different: the allocator, the hashing and the choice of
implementation strategy from key_t and val_t are the same. Use this macro
when the whole useful life of the map is one block, and chmap_construct(3)
when the caller returns the handle, stores it, or destroys it somewhere
that the compiler cannot see.

chmap_declare_scoped(3) followed by chmap_init(3) is the two-step form;
use it for a map whose configuration is decided inside the scope rather
than at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the map uses
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails; use
chmap_declare_scoped(3) with chmap_init(3) when the caller must handle a
failure
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
Uses the default allocator and CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16)
buckets
Scope exit destroys the map and clears the handle, so an explicit
chmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope stays correct, and the cleanup at
scope exit then does nothing
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
Destroying the map frees the copies of the keys and values that it owns,
but not anything that a stored pointer value points at
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle, and do
not store it anywhere that outlives the scope: either one hands out a
handle whose map is freed at the exit from the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int count_unique(char **words, size_t n) {
    chmap_construct_scoped(seen, char *, int);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        chmap_insert(seen, words[i], one);
    }
    return (int)chmap_elem_count(seen);   /* seen is destroyed here */
}
.fi
